  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Apricot Jelly (Guepinia helvelloides) is a small, gelatinous fungus characterized by its distinctive apricot to orange coloring and jelly-like texture. This unusual fungus is found in New England and belongs to a group of fungi with uncertain taxonomic classification.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting body has a distinctive rubbery, jelly-like consistency that is firm yet yielding to pressure",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Apricot Coloration",
      "description": "Displays a characteristic apricot to orange-yellow color that distinguishes it from most other jelly fungi",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Shape",
      "description": "Typically forms small, cup-shaped or brain-like lobes, usually less than 1 inch across",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Apricot Jelly grows on decaying wood and leaf litter in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England.",
